@page "/docs/architecture/sign-in"

@* Sign-in across render modes: sign-in pages are static because a cookie needs the response, how each
   render mode learns who the user is, reading the user one way everywhere, and what the server still has to
   check. The code is the starter's (catalog/blazor, --auth Individual). Patterns only: the framework's own
   details are linked on Microsoft Learn. *@

<ArticlePage Section="architecture" Slug="sign-in">
    <Lead>
        Signing in sets a cookie, and the server reads it on every request. An
        <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive page</Anchor> keeps running between requests, so it has to be
        told who the user is when it starts.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Sign-in pages are static</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Setting a cookie needs an HTTP response, and only a static page or an endpoint has one. So the pages that
            sign in, register and sign out stay static, even in an app that is interactive everywhere else. The starter
            marks its account pages. Created with <Code>--all-interactive</Code>, its <Code>App.razor</Code> leaves the
            render mode off for them:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/Account/Pages/_Imports.razor">@Exclude</Code>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor">@AppMode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Signing out is a form that posts to an endpoint, for the same reason. See Microsoft's
            <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/components/render-modes#static-ssr-pages-in-an-interactive-app" External>Static SSR pages in an interactive app</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">How each render mode learns who the user is</Heading>
        <figure class="site-diagram">
            <div class="site-diagram-scroll ib-scroll">@((MarkupString)Flow)</div>
            <figcaption>The cookie reaches the server with each request, and a circuit reads it once, as it starts. A page in the browser gets the user's name and roles with its first HTML.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Render mode</th><th>The user comes from</th><th>How long it's kept</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Static</td><td>The cookie, read on each request</td><td>One request</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Interactive Server</td><td>The cookie, read when the circuit starts</td><td>As long as the circuit, checked again on a timer</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>WebAssembly, and Auto once it runs in the browser</td><td>The prerendered page, which contains the user's name and roles</td><td>Until the next full page load</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A circuit outlives a request.</strong> If a user signs out in another tab or loses a role, an open
                circuit still has the old user. The starter registers
                <Code>IdentityRevalidatingAuthenticationStateProvider</Code>, which checks the user's security stamp every
                30 minutes and signs the circuit out if it changed. Shorten its <Code>RevalidationInterval</Code> if that's
                too long for your app.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>The browser gets a copy, not the cookie.</strong> The server writes the user's name and roles into
                the prerendered page, and the client reads them back. Register both halves:
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Registering the user's state">@Registration</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Read the user the same way everywhere</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>AuthorizeView</Code>, <Code>[Authorize]</Code> and the cascading <Code>AuthenticationState</Code> work in
            every render mode, so a component that uses them can move between render modes:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@ReadUser</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Don't read <Code>HttpContext</Code> for the user outside a static page. An interactive component has no
            current request, so it's null or out of date there.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What the server still checks</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Pages.</strong> <Code>[Authorize]</Code> on a page stops it rendering for the wrong user, in every
                render mode. On static pages the server redirects to the sign-in page before the page renders. On
                interactive pages, <Code>AuthorizeRouteView</Code> in <Code>Routes</Code> shows the redirect or its "not
                authorized" content.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Endpoints and rows.</strong> In the browser, <Code>[Authorize]</Code> only hides the page, and the
                copy of the user there is for showing a name or hiding a link. The browser sends the cookie with calls
                to the app's own URL, and the endpoint decides. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-browser#checks-that-move-to-the-server">Checks that move to the server</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/create-app">Create an app</Anchor>: <Code>--auth Individual</Code> adds these pages and registrations to the starter.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/security/" External>Blazor authentication and authorization</Anchor>: Microsoft's guide to the pieces named here.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access">Data access by render mode</Anchor>: the stores and endpoints these checks protect.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Exclude = """
        @attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
        """;

    private const string AppMode = """
        <Routes @rendermode="PageRenderMode" />

        @code {
            [CascadingParameter]
            private HttpContext HttpContext { get; set; } = default!;

            // Every page is interactive except those marked [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting].
            private IComponentRenderMode? PageRenderMode =>
                HttpContext.AcceptsInteractiveRouting() ? InteractiveServer : null;
        }
        """;

    private const string Registration = """
        // Program.cs (server)
        builder.Services.AddRazorComponents()
            .AddInteractiveServerComponents()
            .AddInteractiveWebAssemblyComponents()
            .AddAuthenticationStateSerialization();
        builder.Services.AddCascadingAuthenticationState();
        // For the pages on a circuit:
        builder.Services.AddScoped<AuthenticationStateProvider, IdentityRevalidatingAuthenticationStateProvider>();

        // Program.cs (client)
        builder.Services.AddAuthorizationCore();
        builder.Services.AddCascadingAuthenticationState();
        builder.Services.AddAuthenticationStateDeserialization();
        """;

    private const string ReadUser = """
        <AuthorizeView Policy="Sales">
            <Authorized>
                <Button Href="orders/new">New order</Button>
            </Authorized>
        </AuthorizeView>

        @code {
            [CascadingParameter]
            private Task<AuthenticationState>? State { get; set; }

            private async Task<string?> UserName() =>
                State is null ? null : (await State).User.Identity?.Name;
        }
        """;

    // The diagram: a place (browser, server) is a tinted box, what runs there a white one. Colors are in site.css.
    private const string Flow = """
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 150" role="img" aria-label="The browser stores the sign-in cookie and sends it with each request. The server reads the user from it for each static page, and once when a circuit starts. For a page in the browser, the server sends the user's name and roles back inside the page's HTML.">
            <defs><marker id="site-diagram-arrow-d" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path class="site-diagram-head" d="M0 0 8 4 0 8z"/></marker></defs>
            <rect class="site-diagram-place" x="1" y="1" width="220" height="148" rx="8"/>
            <text class="site-diagram-label" x="14" y="24">Browser</text>
            <rect class="site-diagram-node" x="16" y="40" width="190" height="40" rx="6"/>
            <text x="111" y="65" text-anchor="middle">Sign-in cookie</text>
            <rect class="site-diagram-node" x="16" y="96" width="190" height="40" rx="6"/>
            <text x="111" y="121" text-anchor="middle">Page in the browser</text>
            <rect class="site-diagram-place" x="400" y="1" width="239" height="148" rx="8"/>
            <text class="site-diagram-label" x="413" y="24">Server</text>
            <rect class="site-diagram-node" x="415" y="40" width="209" height="40" rx="6"/>
            <text x="519" y="65" text-anchor="middle">Static page, or a new circuit</text>
            <rect class="site-diagram-node" x="415" y="96" width="209" height="40" rx="6"/>
            <text x="519" y="121" text-anchor="middle">Prerendered HTML</text>
            <text class="site-diagram-label" x="310" y="52" text-anchor="middle">the cookie</text>
            <path class="site-diagram-line" d="M206 60H413" marker-end="url(#site-diagram-arrow-d)"/>
            <text class="site-diagram-label" x="310" y="108" text-anchor="middle">name and roles</text>
            <path class="site-diagram-line" d="M415 116H208" marker-end="url(#site-diagram-arrow-d)"/>
        </svg>
        """;
}
